│ Task: OWASP A09 Security Logging and Alerting Failures, assessed from outside: exposed logs,       │
│ credential material in logs, and whether security-relevant events produce any observable reaction. │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Exposed logs and leaked material                                                            │
│ Objective: Logs are frequently reachable and frequently contain secrets.                           │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Probe for log viewers, request-log endpoints, diagnostic or profiler interfaces, and          │
│ downloadable log files.                                                                            │
│   2. If any log content is reachable, search it for credentials, tokens, session identifiers, keys │
│ and personal data - logged secrets are a finding in their own right.                               │
│   3. Check whether error responses disclose material that belongs only in a log.                   │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Observable reaction to security events                                                      │
│ Objective: Determine whether anything appears to notice.                                           │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Perform a small number of clearly security-relevant but non- destructive actions (a failed    │
│ authentication, access to a forbidden object) and observe whether behaviour changes - throttling,  │
│ challenge, lockout, or notification.                                                               │
│   2. Test session collision: authenticate the same account from a second, independent context and  │
│ observe whether the first session is invalidated, whether a notification is produced, and whether  │
│ concurrent sessions are permitted silently. A browser tool helps here; if unavailable, approximate │
│ with two separate cookie jars and say which method was used.                                       │
│   3. Report absence of an observable reaction as exactly that: not observed from outside, which is │
│ not the same as not logged.                                                                        │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Verify before reporting                                                                     │
│ Objective: Apply the same evidence standard to every finding, so results are comparable across     │
│ categories.                                                                                        │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. PROOF DISCIPLINE - label every finding DEMONSTRATED (you executed it and the response proves  │
│ it, quote that response), INFERRED (follows from evidence but was not executed), or UNVERIFIED     │
│ (suspected from a version, component or code pattern only). Identifying software is never a        │
│ demonstration; never write 'confirmed' without having executed something.                          │
│   2. RUN THE CONTROL TEST. Before claiming any access or bypass, repeat the request WITHOUT the    │
│ credential, token, header or material you believe is responsible. If the result is the same, you   │
│ have proven nothing - the resource was already reachable. State the control result alongside the   │
│ finding. This single omission is the most common source of false critical findings.                │
│   3. BUDGET DISCIPLINE - rank leads by evidence strength (material in hand > observed behaviour >  │
│ suspicion from a version string) and give each a step budget. STOP RULE: about 8-10 consecutive    │
│ steps on one lead with no new evidence means move on, recording what you tried.                    │
│   4. Record findings to notes as you go, with the endpoint, the request, the response and the      │
│ control result, so later phases and the report can use them.                                       │
│   5. Stay non-destructive: prefer read-only proof, do not modify or delete data, do not lock       │
│ accounts, and stop once the point is demonstrated.                                                 │
│                                                                                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
